WEBVTT

00:00:00.510 --> 00:00:02.450
Analysis starts with a useful question.

00:00:02.450 --> 00:00:04.520
A vague question produces vague analysis.

00:00:04.520 --> 00:00:06.280
If someone asks, what is going on,

00:00:06.280 --> 00:00:09.805
the analyst may collect a large amount of information without knowing which evidence

00:00:09.805 --> 00:00:10.310
matters.

00:00:10.310 --> 00:00:14.190
A stronger intelligence question identifies the decision to support, the audience,

00:00:14.190 --> 00:00:15.041
the timeframe,

00:00:15.041 --> 00:00:16.800
and the level of detail required.

00:00:16.800 --> 00:00:19.400
That structure turns curiosity into a requirement.

00:00:19.400 --> 00:00:21.915
An intelligence requirement should be tied to a decision.

00:00:21.915 --> 00:00:25.680
For example, a SOC manager may need to decide whether to tune a detection,

00:00:25.680 --> 00:00:26.925
escalate an incident,

00:00:26.925 --> 00:00:29.110
or assign more staff to a recurring alert type.

00:00:29.110 --> 00:00:33.090
A cloud engineer may need to decide whether a configuration pattern creates meaningful

00:00:33.090 --> 00:00:33.520
risk.

00:00:33.520 --> 00:00:37.120
An executive may need to decide whether a business process needs attention.

00:00:37.120 --> 00:00:41.000
A legal or compliance team may need a concise explanation of facts, assumptions,

00:00:41.000 --> 00:00:42.105
and response steps.

00:00:42.105 --> 00:00:45.865
The same information may support all of these audiences, but not in the same format.

00:00:45.865 --> 00:00:49.220
Audience matters because different stakeholders need different detail.

00:00:49.220 --> 00:00:53.335
Technical teams may need evidence tables, timestamps, indicators, data sources,

00:00:53.335 --> 00:00:54.405
and confidence notes.

00:00:54.405 --> 00:00:58.286
Leaders may need key judgments, implications, options, and risk tradeoffs.

00:00:58.286 --> 00:01:01.710
Incident commanders may need what changed, what is known, what is uncertain,

00:01:01.710 --> 00:01:03.320
and what decision is needed now.

00:01:03.320 --> 00:01:05.870
A good analyst does not simply write everything they know.

00:01:05.870 --> 00:01:07.718
They shape the product to the decision.

00:01:07.718 --> 00:01:08.840
Timeframe also matters.

00:01:08.840 --> 00:01:12.900
A five-minute incident update is not the same as a two-week strategic assessment.

00:01:12.900 --> 00:01:16.640
Urgent analysis may need to state preliminary judgments and confidence clearly.

00:01:16.640 --> 00:01:20.730
Longer analysis may include more source evaluation, alternative hypotheses,

00:01:20.730 --> 00:01:21.880
and evidence review.

00:01:21.880 --> 00:01:25.622
Both can be professional if the analyst is clear about limitations and avoids

00:01:25.622 --> 00:01:26.212
overclaiming.

00:01:26.212 --> 00:01:28.390
Clarifying the question prevents wasted effort.

00:01:28.390 --> 00:01:31.450
Before collecting more data, ask what decision is being supported,

00:01:31.450 --> 00:01:33.795
who will use the answer, when the answer is needed,

00:01:33.795 --> 00:01:36.800
what level of confidence is required, what sources are authorized,

00:01:36.800 --> 00:01:38.880
and what would make the answer actionable.

00:01:38.880 --> 00:01:40.860
These questions do not slow analysis down.

00:01:40.860 --> 00:01:44.000
They prevent the team from spending hours on information that does not help.

00:01:44.000 --> 00:01:46.830
A strong requirement is not a bureaucratic formality.

00:01:46.830 --> 00:01:49.110
It is the anchor that keeps analysis relevant.

00:01:49.110 --> 00:01:53.130
It helps the analyst decide what to collect, what to ignore, how to organize evidence,

00:01:53.130 --> 00:01:54.350
how much detail to include,

00:01:54.350 --> 00:01:56.180
and how to communicate uncertainty.

00:01:56.180 --> 00:02:00.720
Good analysis begins when the question is clear enough that evidence can be judged against

00:02:00.720 --> 00:02:00.730
it.
